WEBVTT

00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:12,860
All right. Hi, everybody. This is Michelle with Conor's Clinic and I'm having the great opportunity

00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:18,140
to interview a patient of ours. She's been with us for going on two years now. Her name is

00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:25,980
Christina Kranz. Hi, Christina. Hello. Thank you so much for being here with us. I'd love for you

00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:33,820
to start out by giving us just a small introduction on who you are, kind of what was your diagnosis

00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:39,020
when you started with us, and anything else that you'd like to do just to kind of profess who you

00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:44,780
are to potential patients and current patients who are watching this. Well, thank you for having

00:00:44,780 --> 00:00:53,100
me today. It was really honored to be asked and it's been a journey. So let me just introduce who I am

00:00:53,100 --> 00:01:01,340
and then I'd love to share a little bit about where I am right now. I'm a 35 year old single woman.

00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:07,740
I'm a nurse midwife and have worked in hospital settings and home settings. Alternative care has

00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:14,460
been part of my background, um, car practice, acupuncture, those kinds of things. I was diagnosed with

00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:20,700
endometrial cancer in January of 2018 and kind of shocked by it because I lived a really healthy

00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:28,380
lifestyle and just thought I'm the health care provider. I'm not a patient. So I live in

00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:35,500
Western New York State, grew up with a big family and on a farm. I live the outdoors. I love being

00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:40,940
creative, making pottery. And I love caring for people and I love the Lord Jesus Christ. So

00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:48,780
that's kind of who I am and cancer has rocked my world, stretched me, grown me, made me a different

00:01:48,780 --> 00:01:54,780
person, a different provider, much more compassionate to people who are suffering and I've seen my god

00:01:54,780 --> 00:01:59,180
in a completely different way. He's been me the whole way. So that's a bit of who I am.

00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:05,420
That's perfect. So being that you've had a history of being alternatively minded,

00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:14,540
what was it like for you to receive that diagnosis given that most people in the alternative spectrum

00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:21,740
they're wanting to be healthy because they don't want cancer to come in the future but often most of

00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:28,300
them haven't considered the what if. What would I do if? And so when you get that diagnosis,

00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:33,820
you can be faced with some pretty big decisions and some emotional sways because there's so much fear

00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:38,860
that surrounds it. So I'm curious to know what was it like for you to make those decisions moving

00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:49,040
forward after you were diagnosed? I had watched two of my grandparents through treatments for cancer

00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,640
and I had also had a couple of friends who had done alternative treatments for cancer. So I'd

00:02:56,640 --> 00:03:04,560
kind of seen both firsthand. Yes, I was shocked that I had cancer and you know, my first thought was,

00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:08,800
oh my goodness, I'm going to go through treatments. I'm going to lose all my hair and then I kind of

00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:17,920
paused, you know, and thought, oh wait a second. Right, I could do something different. And I think part

00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:25,840
of the process was trying to decide if the people around me were supportive. So I found my family

00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:32,640
very, very supportive and I think that helped me a lot in making the big decisions of how to use

00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:38,880
conventional treatments in a supportive way but really focusing on alternative treatments.

00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:47,040
So I guess I really had to ask myself, did I really believe that alternative treatments

00:03:47,040 --> 00:03:55,520
were as good as I'd always thought for the big, the big one for cancer? So yes, I was prepared to

00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:03,280
embrace it but I had a lot of questions in the process. Yeah, as you began to move forward, how was it

00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:10,000
blending your primary use of alternative medicine or your desire to use that with maintaining a

00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:20,780
relationship with a traditional doctor or an oncological team? It's been a little touch-and-go

00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:29,740
but I have to say I have had some wonderful doctors in conventional oncology. Sometimes I've left

00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:35,180
the office feeling a lot of doubt. They've instilled that doubt in me and all of their questions.

00:04:35,180 --> 00:04:40,780
Other visits, they've been very affirming. One doctor said to me, I'm the vice president, you're the

00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:46,860
president and the next time he's like, well, so here's my plan. I'm hoping you're going to go along with it.

00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:56,220
So I usually just maintain that I don't make big decisions in the office and I think that they've come

00:04:56,220 --> 00:05:05,980
to respect that. So for myself, I also just purport myself. I am a health care professional. I was

00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:12,380
trained in nursing so I have a language to share with them and then I usually just say, but I appreciate

00:05:12,380 --> 00:05:20,620
science and some science is showing me that conventional therapy isn't enough. And so I usually just

00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:28,700
sound really confident in the office and then I leave and I say to my mom, oh my goodness, I have to

00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:34,620
like offload the things that they've said to me otherwise they keep rolling around in my mind.

00:05:34,620 --> 00:05:42,860
Well, this could blow up someday. Do you feel comfortable in giving an example of that,

00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:48,700
kind of the difficulty that you've had to traverse? You certainly don't have to name names. I want to

00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:53,580
protect and honor that relationship that you have with those practitioners. And so I want to make

00:05:53,580 --> 00:05:58,700
sure that we're keeping everything above board. But can you give an example because I'm sure that you

00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:04,860
wouldn't be alone in this? Sure. So you mean an example of something that was said that I've had

00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:11,980
to disagree with? Either that or a tough conversation where in the office you were really having to weigh out

00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:18,780
what it is that you believe in your heart with just navigating that conversation with somebody who's

00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:30,940
claiming or is believed to be an expert within that field? Sure. I would say there was a time when I

00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:39,180
was declining to do MRIs every three months, which is what their request was. And I instead had

00:06:39,180 --> 00:06:47,660
decided to use the IVG test with Dr. Connors and ultrasound because it didn't include the

00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:57,020
gallenium dye. And the NP who saw me during this particular visit basically came in the room with an

00:06:57,020 --> 00:07:04,460
agenda and she told me you need an MRI. You really should do a histroscopy and a biopsy and she told

00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:10,380
this to me like three times. She didn't even ask me, did I have questions that day? I was really taking

00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:15,900
a back and kind of felt bullied. And then she was getting ready to leave the room and said, well if you

00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:21,420
have any questions you know you can always call. And I just all of a sudden realized that I needed to

00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:25,980
like speak up and stop her and so I said, well actually I do have some things to say. And I was

00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:34,140
able to just communicate a couple things that you know communicated that I was thoughtful, that I was

00:07:35,500 --> 00:07:40,860
thinking about this, that I wasn't wearing rosy-colored glasses and saying don't worry I'm just going

00:07:40,860 --> 00:07:48,140
to get well. I know I'm going to get well. I basically had to reframe that I'm a thoughtful conscientious

00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:56,940
person and the things I chose were different than hers but I was still watching what was going on and

00:07:56,940 --> 00:08:04,380
making my decisions based on what my values were. And she didn't really give a lot of honor and

00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:12,140
affirmation to that but I had to say it and I think that it helped her to maybe just realize that I

00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:17,260
was choosing something different and not being neglectful.

00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:28,620
Yeah that's great. It wasn't really a lot of resolve but you know I had to speak up because otherwise

00:08:28,620 --> 00:08:34,780
she was going in the room saying Christina's just saying no to everything I'm offering and instead I

00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:40,620
had to reframe it and say I'm actually saying yes to things you're not offering. Right I think

00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:47,500
that's good and that's clearing whether or not your conversation ultimately changed her as a human

00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:53,820
being moving forward maybe even the biggest part of that conversation was for you in that you

00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:59,980
needing to say something with love and grace that was trying to bridge that gap or to connect

00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:04,220
the two sides together was allowing you to walk away feeling like you're doing everything that you

00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:13,900
can to maintain that healthy relationship. Sure I think that's true. Yeah. How has traditional medicine

00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:24,480
been helpful for you? I have utilized a progesterone therapy which is a prescription medication

00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:35,360
that has been the main conventional treatment I have used and then imaging so I maintain a working

00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:47,200
relationship with my oncologist for those two services primarily. Okay. Being that you chose you

00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:54,160
did your research and chose to come to work with Connors Clinic. How did Connors Clinic provide you

00:09:54,880 --> 00:10:00,000
the elements that you were looking for within the alternative spectrum? I know that you are

00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:06,560
well researched and that you really took your time to make this decision prayerfully and academically

00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:13,040
and so how did how did we as a clinic come alongside you the way that you felt you needed?

00:10:15,490 --> 00:10:24,210
I felt like Connors Clinic has always treated me like a person and a person with knowledge and opinions

00:10:24,210 --> 00:10:31,570
and a spirit and emotions and a physical body and a relational body and few other providers

00:10:31,570 --> 00:10:36,850
offer that kind of care so I have felt like Connors Clinic has honored me as a whole being.

00:10:38,210 --> 00:10:44,770
I also feel like the education and the community be it the Zoom calls or the Facebook group

00:10:44,770 --> 00:10:51,010
or just being able to go to the website and look up like modified citrus pectin. What's it for?

00:10:51,970 --> 00:10:57,810
Why do I take it? How do I take it? There are just so many resources even on the webpage and

00:10:59,250 --> 00:11:06,290
I don't know you've all been wonderfully encouraging in the process even if my news has been hard news.

00:11:06,290 --> 00:11:15,090
You've just kind of sat with me during that and then given me hope like okay let's do another

00:11:15,090 --> 00:11:24,050
distance kinesiology and see what the next answer is and to me that just offers up a lot of hope

00:11:24,050 --> 00:11:30,290
because there's always something else to explore and find that's going on in my body that might give

00:11:30,290 --> 00:11:37,730
a clue as to why things are going well or why things are going poorly. So I feel like it's been

00:11:37,730 --> 00:11:45,340
multi-level kind of care that I've received from Connors Clinic. Well I'm glad to hear that.

00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:54,620
Well last question in this segment if there was something or some things you can have them be plural

00:11:54,620 --> 00:12:00,460
to messages that you would like to provide for current patients that we have or potential patients

00:12:01,020 --> 00:12:06,300
something that's been on your heart that you'd want to share I want to give you the opportunity

00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:13,980
to dispense that information and you know maybe provide some people are looking for encouragement or

00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:19,980
just a different way to look at things just some of that guidance and wisdom that I know that you've

00:12:19,980 --> 00:12:26,860
gained over the years that you've been working with us. Sure I would say the first thing is that when

00:12:26,860 --> 00:12:35,900
I was first diagnosed I realized the culture of fear that surrounds oncology and I felt that from

00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:45,180
my oncologist I felt it from the radiologist stirring imaging and it's like hurry up and make decisions

00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:53,420
fast or you don't even have time to make decisions here's the plan sign on the dotted line and I

00:12:54,380 --> 00:13:00,380
I guess the Lord just gave me that perception right away that there's a lot of fear-based decision-making

00:13:01,100 --> 00:13:07,660
and there's just this little truth in the Bible that says that perfect love casts out fear and I

00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:15,900
realized that fear shouldn't be making any of my decisions for me because love should cast out

00:13:15,900 --> 00:13:23,340
fear and then I should be able to make decisions in wisdom. So I have just I guess gained the awareness

00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:29,500
that making a decision out of fear is usually going to be heard and regretted later I don't make

00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:37,900
decisions in the office if I get new imaging results and they give me a plan I usually just say okay

00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:43,900
I'll give it some thought and I'll get back to you so my encouragement to anybody who is in the decision

00:13:43,900 --> 00:13:49,900
making process which is kind of continuously but particularly if you're first diagnosed or trying to

00:13:49,900 --> 00:14:02,620
decide even on alternative care to to just pause and to identify fears and to let the Holy Spirit

00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:10,860
rid those fears instead be surrounded by God's love that casts out fears and then make sound decisions

00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:18,300
that would be my first encouragement and it's really really easy to get sucked into make this decision now

00:14:19,100 --> 00:14:25,260
but just kind of have these antennas up like fear is not a great place to be making decisions

00:14:26,060 --> 00:14:35,740
and the second thing I'd say is to ask for wisdom and after I spoke with Dr. Connors the first time

00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:42,940
ever I did my interview with him and he just gave me this wonderful piece of advice that I have done

00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:49,580
over and over and over and it is ask God for wisdom about what direction to take because

00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:57,020
he's going to give you the answer he says ask for wisdom and I will give it to you liberally and

00:14:57,020 --> 00:15:03,340
without scolding you that's in James and over and over I ask for wisdom I ask for wisdom before I

00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:08,060
go to appointments I ask for wisdom before I call Connors Clinic and say hey can you do distance

00:15:08,060 --> 00:15:13,660
kinesiology for me I ask for wisdom sometimes about fixing my food is this a good thing for me to

00:15:13,660 --> 00:15:17,980
be eating today or have I eaten this too many days in a row maybe I need to switch things up

00:15:20,140 --> 00:15:25,180
I just ask for wisdom about the littlest things now because I understand he really wants me to come

00:15:25,180 --> 00:15:28,940
asking for wisdom so whether you're making decisions that are a little or big

00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:35,180
ask he doesn't ever scold us for asking and he wants to give it to us liberally

00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:42,460
and the third thing I would say is I would never have guessed it actually we think well for the

00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:57,950
last two years but I am thank you that's wonderful well Christina thank you so much for answering

00:15:57,950 --> 00:16:04,350
these questions for me and for all of us who will be watching in the future what a blessing

00:16:04,350 --> 00:16:10,190
you have been blessing as a patient to our clinic and we're looking forward to many years ahead

00:16:10,750 --> 00:16:19,470
so thank you for talking with us today you're welcome it's been my pleasure

